## Runtime unblock handoff for wave1 / wave4

For wave1 `74f18b72-19e7-4ca0-a3ac-4b3593d0975e` and wave4 `7633e788-e98f-40d6-9cec-52395042d5ef`: **registered Edge worked even though Get-Command msedge and require.resolve(playwright/puppeteer) failed.** This is a verified local-browser method, not a package/registry/credential workaround. Run only your existing bounded runtime scenarios; do not redo source discovery or spawn agents.

### Exact Edge invocation used

```powershell
$p = Start-Process -FilePath msedge -ArgumentList '--headless=new','--disable-gpu','--no-first-run','--no-default-browser-check','--remote-debugging-port=9415',"--user-data-dir=$PWD\.qa-wave5-browser",'about:blank' -PassThru -ErrorAction Stop
Write-Output "Owned browser PID=$($p.Id)"
Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:9415/json/version
```

Windows registered-app resolution through Start-Process found Edge156.0.4314.58. **Substitute your own fresh profile directory inside your approved worktree and unique unused debug/server ports. Record your PID. Never omit --user-data-dir, reuse wave5's profile/IDs/PIDs, or attach to an existing user browser.** If invocation fails, report that failure; no install or registry/config change.

### Artifact + loopback runtime

Wave5 used:

```powershell
gh run download 41750582447 --name score-king-web --dir .qa-wave5-dist
```

Run41750582447 / artifact13285139626 belongs **only** to SHA `e71c744b1518e6ec6d90e22a0061936fd7e090a9`. Verify your source head first. For a newer build choose the matching successful exact-head CI artifact; don't present e71 execution as execution of newer main. Download into your own fresh worktree-local scratch directory, not another session's files. No npm restoration needed.

This Node-built-ins static server is the method used (tighter resolved-root guard shown here). Substitute directory and unique port. Run as an **attached async PowerShell tool command with a recorded shellId**:

```powershell
node --input-type=module -e 'import http from "node:http";import fs from "node:fs";import path from "node:path";const root=path.resolve(".qa-wave5-dist");http.createServer((req,res)=>{const name=decodeURIComponent(req.url.split("?")[0]);let f=path.resolve(root,"."+name);if(f!==root&&!f.startsWith(root+path.sep)){res.writeHead(403).end();return;}if(!fs.existsSync(f)||fs.statSync(f).isDirectory())f=path.join(root,"index.html");const mime={".html":"text/html",".js":"text/javascript",".css":"text/css",".svg":"image/svg+xml",".png":"image/png",".webmanifest":"application/manifest+json"};res.setHeader("Content-Type",mime[path.extname(f)]||"application/octet-stream");res.end(fs.readFileSync(f));}).listen(9416,"127.0.0.1",()=>console.log("artifact ready"));'
```

Verify the owned loopback URL responds and the actual browser renders the entry. It is ephemeral QA execution, not production code/config. No real services/accounts or credential reads.

### Raw CDP: no imported packages

Node's built-in WebSocket handled CDP. Run this JS via a PowerShell single-quoted here-string piped to `node --input-type=module` (opening marker and closing marker must each be on their own line). Substitute your ports:

```javascript
const info=await (await fetch('http://localhost:9415/json/version')).json();
const ws=new WebSocket(info.webSocketDebuggerUrl);
await new Promise(r=>ws.addEventListener('open',r));
let seq=0;
const pending=new Map();
ws.onmessage=e=>{
  const v=JSON.parse(e.data);
  if(!v.id)return;
  const p=pending.get(v.id);
  if(!p)return;
  pending.delete(v.id);
  v.error?p.reject(v.error):p.resolve(v.result);
};
const c=(method,params={},sessionId)=>new Promise((resolve,reject)=>{
  const id=++seq;
  pending.set(id,{resolve,reject});
  ws.send(JSON.stringify({id,method,params,sessionId}));
});
const {browserContextId}=await c('Target.createBrowserContext');
const {targetId}=await c('Target.createTarget',{url:'about:blank',browserContextId});
const {sessionId}=await c('Target.attachToTarget',{targetId,flatten:true});
await c('Page.enable',{},sessionId);
await c('Network.enable',{},sessionId);
await c('Network.setBlockedURLs',{urls:['https://*','wss://*']},sessionId);
await c('Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride',{
  width:320,height:900,deviceScaleFactor:1,mobile:false
},sessionId);
await c('Page.navigate',{url:'http://127.0.0.1:9416/'},sessionId);
const ev=async expression=>{
  const out=await c('Runtime.evaluate',{
    expression,returnByValue:true,awaitPromise:true
  },sessionId);
  if(out.exceptionDetails)throw Error(JSON.stringify(out.exceptionDetails));
  return out.result.value;
};
console.log({browserContextId,targetId,sessionId});
// Poll the actual required editor/field before measuring or interacting.
console.log(await ev('document.body.innerText'));
// Execute your existing bounded synthetic scenarios here.
ws.close();
```

This uses the proven wave5 setup, with request blocking moved **before app navigation**. Remain exclusively on the owned loopback origin; HTTPS/WSS blocking is not a general allowlist for arbitrary HTTP sites. Never enter OneDrive/live/nearby or real services. Fresh profile plus Target.createBrowserContext isolates browser storage. Reconnecting a later script: attach to your saved targetId and use its **new** sessionId; prior flattened session IDs belong to the old socket.

### Safe fixtures / evidence probes

After the app initializes IndexedDB, Runtime.evaluate an awaited Promise opening **only this fresh context's** `indexedDB.open('score-king',2)`. Transaction `players/games/rounds` and put valid synthetic fixtures matching current `src/lib/types.ts` / `src/lib/storage/db.ts`; use your own distinctive IDs/names. Close the fixture DB connection on transaction completion and reload/navigate `/play/<synthetic-id>` so Svelte loads them. Never clear/delete real data, transplant profiles, or use paired-bug-bash / game d439384a-4cfd-45eb-80a1-2477a1552044. No schema/accounting/rule changes.

Proven probes:

- Theme: Runtime.evaluate `document.documentElement.dataset.theme='light'`; repeat dark. Metrics width320/390/1280, height900, scale1.
- Editor scope: `document.querySelector('.save-round')?.closest('.card')`; inspect text, control values/classes, getBoundingClientRect and clientWidth/scrollWidth.
- AX: `await c('Accessibility.getFullAXTree',{},sessionId)` gives role/name/value/properties. This is AX evidence, **not a real screen-reader pass**.
- Interactions: DOM click or set field value then dispatch bubbling input/change; await actual Svelte DOM state. Reordered rows must be located by player identity, not first-child index.
- Initial fixed-delay missing-card observations were transient and discarded in wave5. Poll readiness; don't report slow initialization as a missing editor.

Preserve exact executed SHA/file:line. No physical-device, touch, live-site or real assistive-tech claims from desktop headless emulation. Execute only the previously assigned bounded cases.

### Stop owned helpers

Close the owned browser using `Browser.close` (disposeBrowserContext also available), and verify debug port closes. Stop the attached server via recorded stop_powershell shellId, then **verify server port closes**: the tool stop left my child Node listener alive. I resolved the exact owned listener using `Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 9416 -State Listen | Select-Object OwningProcess` and stopped **that owned PID only** with `Stop-Process -Id <PID>`. Never kill by executable name or sweep shared ports/profiles. Wave5 debug9415/server9416 are stopped. Untracked synthetic build/profile scratch remains uncommitted; no bulk deletion.
